'''Cardiff West''' ({{Lang-cy|Gorllewin Caerdydd}}) is a  [[List of United Kingdom Parliament constituencies|constituency]]{{#tag:ref|A [[borough constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024]] by [[Alex Barros-Curtis]] of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]].{{#tag:ref|As with all constituencies, the constituency elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election at least every five years.|group= n}}

The constituency retained its name and gained wards as part of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]] and under the [[List of parliamentary constituencies in Wales#Final recommendations|June 2023 final recommendations]] of the [[Boundary Commission for Wales]] for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election]].<ref name="Final proposals report">{{Cite book |url=https://bcomm-wales.gov.uk/sites/bcomm/files/review/E02859434_Boundary%20Commission%20Wales%202023_English_Web%20Accessible_V03.pdf |title=2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituencies - The 2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituencies in Wales |date=28 June 2023 |publisher=[[Boundary Commission for Wales]]}}</ref>

==Constituency profile==
Population areas within the constituency include Riverside, Pontcanna, St Fagans and Ely. There are some [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] and [[Plaid Cymru]]-leaning areas in the north of the seat such as Creigiau and St Fagans, Pentyrch and Radyr, but the bulk of the seat comprises districts towards the centre of Cardiff such as Caerau, Canton, Ely and Riverside which are very strongly Labour.

==History==
A traditionally safe [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] seat, represented for 33 years by [[George Thomas, 1st Viscount Tonypandy|George Thomas]] (who became Speaker in 1976 and was re-elected without party affiliation in 1979). It has returned a [[Conservative Party(UK)|Conservative]] only once, in the Tories' landslide year of 1983, when [[Stefan Terlezki]] became the MP.

Labour regained the seat at the next general election in 1987, when [[Rhodri Morgan]] was elected. After the creation of the [[Welsh Assembly Government]], Morgan stepped down from his Westminster seat in 2001 to serve as leader of [[Welsh Labour]] and [[First Minister for Wales]]. [[Kevin Brennan (politician)|Kevin Brennan]] retained the seat for Labour on Morgan's retirement from Westminster politics. Following the announcement of the date of the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]] Brennan anounced his retirement from politics.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour MP Kevin Brennan says he is standing down |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cl77djl4vqzo |work=[[BBC News]] |date=27 May 2024 |access-date=2 July 2024 }}</ref>

==Boundaries==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Cardiff West (UK Parliament constituency) 2010}}|frame=yes|frame-width=250|text=Map of current boundaries}}
Until 2024 Cardiff West was entirely within the boundaries of the City of Cardiff, though in 2024 (as a result of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies|2023 Review of UK constituences]]) it gained the [[Rhondda Cynon Taf]] ward of [[Pontyclun]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.itv.com/news/wales/2024-05-22/mp-seat-changes-in-wales-new-boundaries-and-constituency-names-to-be-used |title=MP seat changes in Wales: New boundaries and constituency names to be used for first time |work=ITV News |date=22 May 2024 |access-date=2024-05-31}}</ref>

'''1950–1974''': The County Borough of Cardiff wards of [[Canton (Cardiff electoral ward)|Canton]], [[Ely (Cardiff electoral ward)|Ely]], [[Grangetown (Cardiff electoral ward)|Grangetown]], [[Llandaff]], and [[Riverside (Cardiff electoral ward)|Riverside]].

'''1974–1983''': The County Borough of Cardiff wards of Canton, Ely, Llandaff, Grangetown, Plasmawr, and Riverside.

'''1983–2010''': The City of Cardiff wards of [[Caerau (Cardiff electoral ward)|Caerau]], Canton, Ely, [[Fairwater (Cardiff electoral ward)|Fairwater]], Llandaff, Radyr and St Fagans, and Riverside.

'''2010–2024''': The City of Cardiff wards of Caerau, Canton, [[Creigiau & St Fagans|Creigiau and St Fagans]], Ely, Fairwater, Llandaff, [[Pentyrch]], [[Radyr (electoral ward)|Radyr]], and Riverside.

'''2024–present''': The City of Cardiff wards of Caerau, Canton, Ely, Fairwater, Llandaff, Pentyrch and St Fagans, Radyr, and Riverside, and the Rhondda Cynon Taf wards of Pont-y-clun Central, Pont-y-clun East, and Pont-y-clun West.

