'''Llanelli''' is a [[constituency (UK)|constituency]] of the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]].{{#tag:ref|A [[county constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} From 1918 to 1970 the official spelling of the constituency name was '''Llanelly'''. It elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election. Since 2005, it is currently represented by [[Nia Griffith]] of the [[Welsh Labour Party|Labour Party]]. 

The [[Llanelli (Senedd constituency)|Llanelli Senedd constituency]] was created with the same boundaries in 1999 (as an Assembly constituency).

The constituency is to retain its name and gain wards, as part of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]] and under the [[List of parliamentary constituencies in Wales#Final recommendations|June 2023 final recommendations]] of the [[Boundary Commission for Wales]] for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election]].<ref name="Final proposals report">{{Cite book |url=https://bcomm-wales.gov.uk/sites/bcomm/files/review/E02859434_Boundary%20Commission%20Wales%202023_English_Web%20Accessible_V03.pdf |title=2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituencies - The 2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituencies in Wales |date=28 June 2023 |publisher=[[Boundary Commission for Wales]]}}</ref>

==Boundaries==
'''1918'''
The constituency was established in 1918, as a division of [[Carmarthenshire]], located in the south east of the county. This area had, until 1918, been the southern part of the constituency of [[East Carmarthenshire (UK Parliament constituency)|East Carmarthenshire]].

It consisted of the then local authority areas of the [[Municipal Borough]] of Llanelly; the [[Urban district (Great Britain and Ireland)|Urban District]]s of [[Ammanford]], [[Burry Port]] and [[Cwmamman]]; the [[Rural District]]s of Lanelly and part of [[Llandeilo|Llandilofawr]] (namely the civil parishes of [[Betws, Carmarthenshire|Betws]], [[Llandybie]] and [[Quarter Bach]], and Ward I of the civil parish of Llandilo Rural)<ref>[[Representation of the People Act 1918]], Ninth Schedule, Part II, Parliamentary Counties: Wales and Monmouthshire.</ref>

The division bordered [[Carmarthen (UK Parliament constituency)|Carmarthen]] to the west and north, [[Brecon and Radnor (UK Parliament constituency)|Brecon and Radnor]] to the north east, [[Neath (UK Parliament constituency)|Neath]] to the east, [[Gower (UK Parliament constituency)|Gower]] to the south east and the sea to the south.

'''1950'''
In the next redistribution of constituencies in Wales, which took effect in 1950, the northern boundary of the constituency was slightly altered. Llanelly no longer bordered [[Brecon and Radnor (UK Parliament constituency)|Brecon and Radnor]] and Gower was extended north and took over the part of the 1918 Neath constituency that had previously adjoined Carmarthenshire. The constituency area continued to include the same local authorities as in 1918 (apart from a spelling change to [[Llandeilo|Llandilo]] for the part RDC included):<ref>Schedule 1, Representation of the People Act 1948, c. 65.</ref>
*The borough of Llanelly;
*The urban districts of Ammanford, Burry Port and Cwmamman;
*The rural district of Llanelly and the parish of Bettws in the rural district of Llandilo.

At the [[1970 United Kingdom general election|1970 general election]] the official spelling of the constituency name was altered to '''Llanelli'''.<ref>{{cite news|title=Over 1,800 candidates for 630 seats in Commons |newspaper=[[The Times]] |date=9 June 1970 |page=10}}</ref> This followed the change in name of both the borough and rural district in 1966.<ref>The borough was renamed on 4 March 1966 and the rural district on 3 May 1966 {{cite book|title=Census of England and Wales 1961. County Report: Carmarthenshire |publisher=[[HMSO]]}}</ref><ref>{{London Gazette |issue=43915 |date=4 March 1966 |page=2444 }}</ref><ref>Llanelli is the spelling Craig uses in his compilation of results from 1950–1973, despite the entry in his book on parliamentary boundaries for the 1950 redistribution following the statute in using Llanelly.</ref>

'''1974'''
The constituency appears to have been unchanged by the redistribution. The local authorities remained the same (apart from spelling changes):
*The borough of Llanelli;
*the urban districts of Ammanford, Burry Port and Cwmamman;
*the rural district of Llanelli and the parish of Bettws in the rural district of Llandeilo.<ref name=1970order>The Parliamentary Constituencies (Wales) Order 1970 (1970/1675).</ref>

The substantial [[Local Government Act 1972|local government changes]] which took effect in 1974 did not affect this redistribution as it used the boundaries as they existed in November 1970 to construct parliamentary constituencies.<ref name=1970order/>

'''1983'''
The redistribution altered the constituency by 8.4%. 96.2% of the new constituency had been in the old one. 3.8% of the electors came from the former Carmarthen constituency.

The area now formed part of the new county of [[Dyfed]]. The district level local government units contained in the constituency were the Borough of Llanelli and Wards 2–6 and 9 of the Borough of [[Dinefwr Borough Council|Dinefwr]].

'''1997'''
In this redistribution the constituency was reduced so that it covered the same area as the [[Llanelli (district)|Borough of Llanelli]].

{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Llanelli (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|frame-height=250|text=Map of former boundaries}}
'''2010'''
After the United Kingdom general election in May 2010, the constituency has comprised the Carmarthenshire County electoral divisions of [[Bigyn (electoral ward)|Bigyn]], [[Burry Port]], [[Bynea]], [[Dafen, Carmarthenshire|Dafen]], [[Elli (electoral division)|Elli]], [[Felinfoel]], [[Glanymor]], [[Glyn (electoral ward)|Glyn]], [[Hendy]], [[Hengoed, Carmarthenshire|Hengoed]], [[Kidwelly]], [[Llangennech]], [[Llannon]], [[Lliedi (electoral ward)|Lliedi]], [[Llwynhendy]], [[Pembrey]], [[Pontyberem]], Swiss Valley, [[Trimsaran]], [[Tycroes]] and [[Tyisha]].

The constituency includes the whole of 9 Carmarthenshire [[Local government in Wales#Communities|communities]] ([[Kidwelly]]; [[Llanedi]]; [[Llanelli]]; [[Llanelli Rural]];  [[Llangennech]]; [[Llannon]]; [[Pembrey and Burry Port Town]]; [[Pontyberem]]; and [[Trimsaran]]).

